WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.235 --> 00:00:01.859
- [Narrator] Principles For Success,

00:00:01.859 --> 00:00:03.948
an ultra mini-series adventure

00:00:03.948 --> 00:00:06.948
in 30 minutes and in eight episodes.

00:00:07.976 --> 00:00:09.556
Episode Four:

00:00:09.556 --> 00:00:10.389
The Abyss

00:00:12.298 --> 00:00:15.816
We progress forward until
we encounter setbacks.

00:00:15.816 --> 00:00:17.360
Whether or not we get out of them

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.409
and continue forward or spiral downward

00:00:20.409 --> 00:00:22.199
depends on whether or not we're willing

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:24.593
to face the failure objectively

00:00:24.593 --> 00:00:26.313
and make the right decisions to

00:00:26.313 --> 00:00:29.198
turn the loop upward again.

00:00:29.198 --> 00:00:32.347
Something terrible happened to me in 1982,

00:00:32.347 --> 00:00:34.719
when I bet everything on a depression

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:36.178
that never came.

00:00:36.178 --> 00:00:38.758
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:00:38.758 --> 00:00:39.967
(birds chirping)

00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:43.160
The period between 1979 and 1982

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.217
was one of extreme turbulence.

00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:46.884
For the global economy,

00:00:46.884 --> 00:00:47.842
the markets,

00:00:47.842 --> 00:00:48.759
and for me.

00:00:49.891 --> 00:00:52.286
And I believed that the U.S. economy,

00:00:52.286 --> 00:00:54.200
with the world economy tied to it,

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.874
was headed toward a catastrophe.

00:00:56.874 --> 00:00:59.982
This view was extremely controversial.

00:00:59.982 --> 00:01:01.873
I wanted the great upside

00:01:01.873 --> 00:01:05.380
and very publicly took a
big risk and was wrong.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:06.644
Dead wrong.

00:01:06.644 --> 00:01:07.888
After a delay,

00:01:07.888 --> 00:01:10.649
the stock market began a big bull market

00:01:10.649 --> 00:01:13.793
that lasted 18 years and the U.S. economy

00:01:13.793 --> 00:01:17.136
enjoyed the greatest growth
period in its history.

00:01:17.136 --> 00:01:17.969
(birds chirping)
This experience was

00:01:17.969 --> 00:01:20.839
like a blow to my head
with a baseball bat.

00:01:20.839 --> 00:01:22.290
I had to cut my losses

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.106
so that my company, Bridgewater,

00:01:24.106 --> 00:01:26.451
was left with one employee:

00:01:26.451 --> 00:01:27.284
Me.

00:01:27.284 --> 00:01:28.608
(door slams shut)

00:01:28.608 --> 00:01:31.414
I was so broke, I had to borrow $4,000

00:01:31.414 --> 00:01:34.099
from my dad to pay my bills,

00:01:34.099 --> 00:01:36.223
but even worse was having to let go

00:01:36.223 --> 00:01:38.973
the people I cared so much about.

00:01:40.031 --> 00:01:41.812
I wondered whether I
should give up my dream

00:01:41.812 --> 00:01:44.523
of working for myself and play it safe

00:01:44.523 --> 00:01:46.570
by working for someone else in a job

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.571
that would require me to put on a tie

00:01:48.571 --> 00:01:50.174
and commute every day.

00:01:50.174 --> 00:01:51.865
Though I knew that, for me,

00:01:51.865 --> 00:01:53.828
taking less risk would mean having

00:01:53.828 --> 00:01:55.879
a less great life.

00:01:55.879 --> 00:01:57.054
Being so wrong,

00:01:57.054 --> 00:01:59.497
and especially being so publicly wrong,

00:01:59.497 --> 00:02:01.691
was painfully humbling.

00:02:01.691 --> 00:02:03.672
I am still shocked and embarrassed

00:02:03.672 --> 00:02:06.105
by how arrogant I was

00:02:06.105 --> 00:02:08.101
in being totally confident

00:02:08.101 --> 00:02:11.242
in a totally incorrect view.

00:02:11.242 --> 00:02:12.075
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:02:12.075 --> 00:02:14.823
Though I'd been right much
more than I'd been wrong,

00:02:14.823 --> 00:02:18.573
I had let one bad bet
erase all my good ones.

00:02:19.795 --> 00:02:21.715
I thought very hard about the relationship

00:02:21.715 --> 00:02:26.097
between risk and reward
and how to manage them,

00:02:26.097 --> 00:02:27.961
but I couldn't see a path forward

00:02:27.961 --> 00:02:30.621
that would give me the rewards I wanted

00:02:30.621 --> 00:02:33.018
without unacceptable risk.

00:02:33.018 --> 00:02:36.506
This kind of experience
happens to everyone.

00:02:36.506 --> 00:02:38.776
It will happen to you.

00:02:38.776 --> 00:02:41.505
You will lose something or someone

00:02:41.505 --> 00:02:43.896
you think you can't live without.

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:47.483
Or you will suffer a
terrible illness or injury.

00:02:47.483 --> 00:02:51.554
Or your career will fall
apart before your eyes.

00:02:51.554 --> 00:02:53.831
You might think that your life is ruined

00:02:53.831 --> 00:02:56.778
and that there's no way to go forward,

00:02:56.778 --> 00:02:58.195
but it will pass.

00:02:59.181 --> 00:03:00.422
I assure you

00:03:00.422 --> 00:03:03.480
that there is always a best path forward

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.600
and you probably just don't see it yet.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.073
You just have to reflect well to find it.

00:03:10.073 --> 00:03:13.234
You have to embrace your reality.

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:15.516
In Episode 5, I'll show you how this

00:03:15.516 --> 00:03:19.325
realization drove me to
wonder how reality works

00:03:19.325 --> 00:03:21.742
and how to best deal with it.

